## Download sources

The images not listed below take their client from the base image or from the
package manager they run (apk, APT, pip, gem, npm or mix). The images below also
download a file while they build. Each file is pinned to the digest its upstream
publishes: a SHA-256, or the SHA-512 that Maven Central and Eclipse publish for
Ivy and Eclipse. Where a second host serves the same bytes, the build falls back
to it, so one host being down does not fail the build
([#1321](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1321),
[#1322](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1322)).
[`fetch-pinned.sh`](../../test/acceptance/clients/fetch-pinned.sh) tries each
source in turn and keeps the first file whose digest matches the pin. It passes
over a source that answers with other bytes as it does one that does not answer,
so a second source never changes what runs. When every source is down, the build
fails. A pin names the first URL only; `make deps-sync` moves the second URL with
it.

| Dockerfile | Download | First source | Second source |
|---|---|---|---|
| [`composer.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/composer.Dockerfile) | Composer {{composer2}} and {{composer1}} phars | `getcomposer.org/download/` | The `composer.phar` asset of Composer's GitHub releases [{{composer2}}](https://github.com/composer/composer/releases/tag/{{composer2}}) and [{{composer1}}](https://github.com/composer/composer/releases/tag/{{composer1}}) |
| [`git-lfs.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/git-lfs.Dockerfile) | Git LFS {{git-lfs}} tarball | GitHub release `git-lfs/git-lfs` | None. No other host serves it; packagecloud holds `.deb` and `.rpm` packages, which are other bytes |
| [`jetbrains.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/jetbrains.Dockerfile) | IntelliJ IDEA {{idea}} tarballs | `download.jetbrains.com` | None. `cache-redirector.jetbrains.com` only redirects to it, and `download-cdn.jetbrains.com` refuses a request without the signed query it adds |
| [`luarocks.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/luarocks.Dockerfile) | Lua {{lua}} tarball | `www.lua.org/ftp/` | [`www.tecgraf.puc-rio.br/lua/ftp/`](https://www.tecgraf.puc-rio.br/lua/ftp/), the PUC-Rio mirror that [lua.org's mirrors page](https://www.lua.org/mirrors.html) names |
| [`luarocks.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/luarocks.Dockerfile) | LuaRocks {{luarocks}} tarball | `luarocks.org/releases/` | [`luarocks.github.io/luarocks/releases/`](https://luarocks.github.io/luarocks/releases/), where `luarocks.org` redirects. It covers `luarocks.org` being down, not GitHub Pages |
| [`maven.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/maven.Dockerfile) | Ivy {{ivy}} jar | `repo.maven.apache.org` | [Google's mirror of Maven Central](https://maven-central.storage-download.googleapis.com/maven2/org/apache/ivy/ivy/{{ivy}}/ivy-{{ivy}}.jar), which [maven.apache.org](https://maven.apache.org/repository/) names |
| [`maven.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/maven.Dockerfile) | Gradle {{gradle}} distribution | `services.gradle.org/distributions/` | [GitHub release `gradle/gradle-distributions` v{{gradle}}](https://github.com/gradle/gradle-distributions/releases/tag/v{{gradle}}), where `services.gradle.org` redirects. It covers the redirector being down, not GitHub |
| [`opkg.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/opkg.Dockerfile) | opkg {{opkg}} tarball | `downloads.yoctoproject.org/releases/opkg/` | [`mirrors.kernel.org/yocto/opkg/`](https://mirrors.kernel.org/yocto/opkg/opkg-{{opkg}}.tar.gz), kernel.org's copy of the Yocto releases |
| [`p2.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/p2.Dockerfile) | Eclipse Platform {{p2}} archives | `archive.eclipse.org` | [`ftp2.osuosl.org/pub/eclipse/`](https://ftp2.osuosl.org/pub/eclipse/eclipse/downloads/), OSU Open Source Lab, on Eclipse's mirror list. It holds only the releases `download.eclipse.org` still holds |
| [`sbt.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/sbt.Dockerfile) | sbt {{sbt}} tgz | GitHub release `sbt/sbt` | None. SDKMAN redirects to the same release, and Maven Central holds no launcher tgz |
| [`vagrant.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/vagrant.Dockerfile) | Vagrant {{vagrant}} `.deb` | `releases.hashicorp.com/vagrant/` | [HashiCorp's apt repository](https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/pool/amd64/main/vagrant_{{vagrant}}-1_amd64.deb) |
| [`vscode.Dockerfile`](../../test/acceptance/clients/vscode.Dockerfile) | VS Code archive | `vscode.download.prss.microsoft.com` | None. `update.code.visualstudio.com` redirects to the same host |

The Puppet image stays on Ruby 3.3 through a Dependabot entry of its own,
because the pinned Puppet cannot extract a module archive on Ruby 3.4
([#1126](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1126)). The
npm 12 image stays on Node 24 LTS the same way: it keeps the prior Node line
beside the newest Node ([decision 0034](../research/decisions/0034-npm.md)).
The `dotnet-10` image stays on the .NET SDK's 10.0 LTS line the same way,
beside the newest line in `dotnet.Dockerfile`
([#1229](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1229)).

The Hex image is the one that Dependabot does not move. `hexpm/elixir` holds a
tag for every combination of Elixir, Erlang/OTP and OS release, about a million,
and Docker Hub answers 504 when Dependabot lists them, even 100 at a time
([#1240](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1240)). So
Dependabot ignores the image, and `deps-refresh.yml` moves it with
`tools/depsync/hex_image.py`. The script takes the newest Elixir release and the
newest OTP and Alpine image that `hexpm/elixir` publishes for it, through Docker
Hub's name-filtered tag API. It pins the tag by digest and never moves a part of
the pin backwards. The move goes up on `deps/hex-image` and needs the same review
as any client image. The official `elixir` image has few tags, but they name only
the OTP major, so the declared pins could no longer name the exact OTP release.
Dependabot also misorders those tags: from `1.20.5-otp-29-alpine` it would
propose `1.20-otp-28-alpine`.

Every base is a stable release. `tools/depsync/sync.py` refuses an acceptance
Dockerfile whose `FROM` tag carries a prerelease marker after its version, such
as `-118.1.beta`, `a1`, `rc1`, `-dev` or `-preview`, and names the file, the tag
and the marker. Variant suffixes such as `-slim`, `-alpine` or `-bookworm` pass.
Dependabot proposes such tags for `python` and `dart`, whose `latest` tag shares
its digest with no full version tag, so `.github/dependabot.yml` ignores their
prereleases by version range
([#1223](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1223),
[#1304](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/1304)).

Purposeful compatibility lanes retain their declared runtimes. Updating a pin
regenerates this inventory, recipe labels and secondary version assertions; it
does not bypass native tests or the review required for acceptance client changes.
[QUAL-01 #792](https://github.com/weaveplatform/artifactweave/issues/792) records
the final merged-main qualification. See [dependency maintenance](dependency-maintenance.md).
